this patch of land 20 kilometers outside  the capital Brasilia used to be the  biggest landfill site in Latin America  last year the dump was closed and the 60  meter high mountain of garbage was  covered with earth construction waste is  still being dumped there although that's  illegal the national waste policy aims  to eradicate landfills and promote  recycling with residual waste either  incinerated or going to sanitary  landfills that protect the environment  but there are still two and a half  thousand open landfill sites across the  country  Lucia Fernandez worked here as a waste  picker for 20 years mainly at night she  supported her four children by selling  anything of value she found they used to  live in a shantytown right beside the  dump we were always in a struggle with  the trucks the bulldozers and with more  than a thousand other people working  here it was survival of the fittest you  took as much as you could get and for  women it was very challenging people  lost their lives working here it's very  tough  at first Lucia Fernandez was very much  against the closure of the garbage dump  she had little faith in the city  authorities pledged to provide jobs for  some 750 trash pickers in new recycling  facilities but now that's where she  works and she's changed her mind  no capping say he do [ __ ] I never  thought I might one day have a real job  me a simple waste picker with no rights  and no social insurance and now I work  here I even have an office  if only it paid a little better it would  be perfect she used to earn four times  as much scavenging on the old dump she  and her colleagues now belonged to a  cooperative that operates one of the  recycling facilities what they earn  depends on how much trash they sought on  average only about 200 euros a month so  far because for now not enough waste is  delivered to the facility Lucia  Fernandez is a supervisor she makes sure  the work is done properly there aren't  enough people working on the 